      modelAnswer: 'A binary search tree (BST) is a binary tree in which every node carries a key, and for any node N, all keys in its left subtree are strictly less than N\'s key, while all keys in its right subtree are strictly greater. This ordering invariant supports efficient search and insertion. To search for a key k, start at the root: if k equals the node\'s key, return success; if k is less, descend into the left child; if greater, descend right; if the child is null, k is not present. Each step halves (on average) the remaining tree, giving average-case O(log n) time. Insertion follows the same path: starting at the root, navigate as if searching; when a null pointer is found, that is the position where the new node belongs, and a fresh node is attached there. The new node becomes a leaf. Deletion is more complex: if the node has no children it is simply removed; if it has one child, that child takes its place; if it has two, its in-order successor (smallest key in the right subtree) is copied into its place, and the successor is then deleted from the right subtree. BSTs degrade to O(n) operations if inserts arrive in sorted order, producing a skewed tree resembling a linked list. Self-balancing variants such as AVL trees and red–black trees maintain O(log n) by performing rotations during insertion and deletion. BSTs are widely used as the basis of ordered maps, range queries and priority-driven data sets where both fast lookup and ordered traversal matter.'
