      q: 'Evaluate the use of queues — linear, circular and priority — in real-world systems.',
      marks: 16, command: 'Evaluate',
      modelAnswer: 'A queue is a First-In-First-Out structure. Variants differ in their internal implementation and their dequeuing policy, and each suits a particular class of problem. Linear queue. Implemented over an array, with a head pointer that advances on dequeue. Each dequeue leaves the slot at the head unused, so over time the queue creeps along the array and eventually appears full when it still has space — this is poor space efficiency. Linear queues are simple and fine for short-lived structures, but rarely chosen in production. Circular queue. The same array is treated as a ring: indices wrap around using modular arithmetic, so dequeued slots are immediately reusable. This gives O(1) enqueue and dequeue with bounded memory and no copying, which makes circular queues the standard choice for fixed-size buffers — audio buffers, OS input/output buffers, network packet buffers between producer and consumer threads, ring loggers for embedded systems. The cost is a small implementation complexity and the need to detect full/empty states (often by leaving one slot deliberately empty, or by maintaining a separate count). Priority queue. Items are dequeued not by arrival order but by an associated priority. Implemented over a binary heap, this gives O(log n) insertion and removal of the highest-priority element. Real-world uses include task scheduling in operating systems (high-priority real-time tasks run before background jobs), shortest-path algorithms such as Dijkstra\'s, A* search in routing and games, event-driven simulations and Huffman coding for data compression. Priority queues sacrifice strict FIFO behaviour for the much more valuable property of "always do the most important thing next". Choosing between them is a trade-off. Linear queues are easy but wasteful; circular queues are space-efficient and excellent for bounded streams; priority queues are essential whenever fairness based on importance, not arrival, is required. Many systems combine them: for example, an operating-system scheduler uses several priority queues (one per priority level), each of which is internally a circular queue, giving both fairness within a priority and overall priority-based service. Used poorly — for example, a priority queue where priorities never change, leading to starvation of low-priority items — queues can fail their purpose, so design must include policies such as ageing or fairness adjustments where needed.'
