      modelAnswer: 'Algorithms operate on data structures; the structure determines which operations are cheap and which are expensive, and so dictates an algorithm\'s asymptotic performance. Often a thoughtful choice of structure transforms an algorithm from impractical to fast, while a poor choice ruins an otherwise good algorithm. Example 1: membership testing. Repeatedly checking whether an item is in a collection of n items takes O(n) with an unsorted array or linked list, O(log n) with a sorted array (binary search) or a balanced BST, and O(1) average with a hash table. A program that performs millions of membership tests therefore runs minutes with a list, seconds with a sorted array and milliseconds with a hash table — the algorithm has not changed, only the data structure. Example 2: shortest path. Dijkstra\'s algorithm finds the shortest path in a graph. With a naive linear search of unvisited vertices it runs in O(V²). Implemented with a binary-heap priority queue it runs in O((V + E) log V). On a road network with millions of vertices, the heap-based version is the difference between an interactive route planner and an overnight batch job. Example 3: ordered output. Producing items in sorted order from a stream of input has cost O(n²) using repeated linear insertion into a list, but O(n log n) using a balanced BST or a heap, because each insertion is O(log n). For large streams this is again the difference between practical and impractical. Example 4: file systems and databases. The B-tree is so suited to disk-resident ordered data that it underpins nearly every modern database index — its design recognises that disk reads are vastly slower than RAM access and structures data to minimise them. The choice also affects memory usage. Arrays are dense but rigid; linked structures use memory per node for pointers but grow on demand; hash tables waste space deliberately to keep load factor low and operations fast. A program with strict memory limits may need to accept O(log n) operations from a B-tree rather than the O(1) of a hash table, simply because the hash table\'s overhead is unaffordable. Choices interact. A linked list with a separate hash index gives O(1) lookup and O(1) insertion at the head — better than either structure alone, at the cost of double the memory. A priority queue can be implemented as a heap (fast updates, slow ordered iteration), a sorted list (slow updates, fast ordered iteration) or a hash table keyed by priority (fast access, no inherent order); the right choice depends on the operation mix. The discipline of choosing the right structure for the access pattern is one of the largest single levers a programmer has over performance. Modern languages provide rich standard libraries precisely so that programmers can mix and match these structures without writing them by hand, but knowing the underlying behaviour is essential to choosing wisely.'
