      marks: 25, command: 'To what extent',
      modelAnswer: 'In a small program, data structure choice is a minor detail; in a large system it can be one of the largest single determinants of both run-time behaviour and the ease of evolving the code. However, like the paradigm question, the answer is "to a substantial extent, but rarely alone". Where data structure choice dominates performance. Asymptotic complexity matters: an algorithm that performs n lookups against an unsorted linked list is O(n²); against a hash table it is O(n) on average. For systems handling millions of records — search engines, social networks, large e-commerce sites — this is the difference between a usable product and one that fails under real load. Memory characteristics matter just as much: cache-friendly contiguous structures (arrays, ring buffers) often outperform asymptotically equivalent linked structures by an order of magnitude on modern hardware because they exploit CPU prefetching. Disk-resident systems live or die by their indexing data structures: B-trees power most relational databases, log-structured merge trees power high-volume key-value stores like LevelDB and Cassandra, and Bloom filters are routinely used to short-circuit expensive disk reads. A poor choice in any of these places does not merely slow the system; it can make the system economically unviable, requiring far more hardware than necessary. Where data structure choice dominates maintainability. The right structure makes algorithms read like prose; the wrong one obscures intent. A queue named exactly that communicates more than a generic list onto whose tail items happen to be appended. Strongly typed collections (List<Customer> rather than a list of generic objects) catch type errors at compile time and serve as documentation. Encapsulated data structures expose only the operations clients need, so internal changes — switching from an array to a balanced tree, say — do not ripple through call sites. Excellent libraries codify these choices: a single line that uses a TreeMap or HashMap communicates the design intent and removes the temptation to roll a custom structure that future maintainers must read and trust. Where choice does not dominate. Even the best data structure will not save a system from poor architecture: monolithic code with tangled dependencies, no separation of concerns, no test coverage, no observability. Empirical studies of long-lived projects consistently identify those higher-level issues as the principal drivers of maintenance cost. Likewise, slow services often hit walls that no data-structure change can move — network latency, GC pauses, lock contention, serialisation costs, third-party API rate limits. Optimising the wrong layer is a classic source of wasted effort, and many junior engineers reach for clever data structures when a profiler would point them at a database round-trip instead. Practical interaction. The choice also interacts with team capability and language: a team comfortable with built-in collections will produce maintainable code that performs well; a team writing custom data structures every project will probably reinvent bugs that the standard library long since solved. Languages matter — Java\'s rich java.util.concurrent and the C++ STL relieve enormous design pressure that programmers in older languages had to bear themselves. Conclusion. Data structure choice has a first-order effect on the performance of large systems, especially at the boundaries (databases, indexes, caches, hot inner loops) where small inefficiencies are multiplied by huge data volumes. It has a secondary but real effect on maintainability, mostly through how clearly it communicates intent and how cleanly it is encapsulated. Yet it is one factor among many; the discipline of choosing well must be matched by good architecture, observability and engineering practice for the system to succeed overall. Excellent engineers know when the data structure is the bottleneck and when it is not, and they do not optimise structures the profiler has not flagged.'
